  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 328 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nThe latest NBM forecast initiated with some isolated pops \nacross northern into eastern portions of the forecast area late \nFriday night into Saturday. This is supported somewhat by the \nNBM ensemble QPF exceedance forecast which has 20 to 30 percent \nchances of QPF>0.01\" from north central into eastern portions of\nthe CWA. This is also supported by the deterministic NAM12 soln\ntonight which develops convection along the nose of a low level\njet, co-located with an area of decent mid level warm air \nadvection. With the NBM forecast capping pops under 20 percent, \nfeel the initialized forecast is plausible ATTM. Model agreement\nbetween the NAM12 and GFS is lacking Saturday with its forecast\nof a warm frontal boundary. The GFS lifts this feature north \nthrough the forecast area Saturday morning with the NAM 12 soln \nstalling this feature along the Nebraska/Kansas border. No \nsurprise, the latest MET and MAV guidance for highs for LBF \nSaturday are 87 and 101 degrees respectively. Given the large \ndiscrepancy between the two models, the NBM is preferred here \nand was utilized for highs Saturday. High pressure aloft, will \nremain anchored over the Four Corners through Sunday. This will \nlead to a second day of very warm temperatures across the \nforecast area with highs reaching the middle to upper 90s. The \nhigh will begin to retrograde slowly west Monday amplifying mid \nlevel flow across the central CONUS. With the high retrograding \nwest, slightly cooler temperatures will develop early next week.\nAfter highs in the 80s and lower 90s Monday and Tuesday, \nreadings will cool into the 80s Wednesday. The threat for \nprecipitation will increase toward the middle of next week as a \nseries of disturbances cross the central plains. ATTM, the best \nchances for precipitation are Tuesday night into Wednesday.",
